Title: ZODIAC WARS: BLOODLINES
Episode: 104, "WHAT THE FOUNDRY KEPT"
Credit: Draft 1 SHORT — PROPOSED, not approved
Author: Prepared for Ω SYD OMEGA 91717 STUDIOS GLOBAL under the founder's direction
Draft date: 2026-11-10
Notes: Cold open and three condensed acts, about 14 script pages of a 50-minute episode, plus a short tag. Season 1, gate KEEPER, the Aries episode. It runs alongside episode 103 (short draft, not approved) and ends a few days after it. Canon this draft respects: Orla Tevane is dead, had a steel hand, and her name is on the lip of the new bell (the film); the Aries tongue is old green bronze and, in Orla's words in the film, "Twelve Houses, twelve tongues, one chime every twelve years, to remind them they swore"; Ossara's count of her in the film is "Eleven thousand pours"; the oath is "say the cost before the pour"; Tamsin pours and Brannock keeps the book (episode 101); the bell is not rung this season before the finale; Ares is a voice only, here from the furnace, and his line in the film is "I pressed Mars toward initiative"; Sentinel is a voice with no body. New in this draft, and the founder's to accept or strike: the foundry's Year One ledger; that the Aries tongue was cast from the name-tags of the Aries dead of the first war, on the order of Ares Valorian, the registry's proposed Age IV regent of Aries, who is named but does not appear; the worn assay tag; the count of eleven thousand four hundred and six; that Orla's eleven thousand pours were her way of counting them; and the last page of her notebook. Whether the other eleven tongues were made the same way is asked and not answered. No new named character. "Say the cost" is said once, by Brannock. Nothing here is filmed, released or playable.

FADE IN:

INT. THE ARES FOUNDRY — THE MASTER'S ROOM — NIGHT

A door that has been shut for weeks. A key turns. It opens on a small room over the furnace hall: a narrow bed, made; a bench under the window; tools hung in order of size; on a peg, a leather harness with buckles for a steel hand. Furnace light comes up red through the floor grating.

TAMSIN OKORO stands in the doorway with the key in her fist. Behind her, BRANNOCK, holding a lantern he does not need.

BRANNOCK
We said a year.

TAMSIN
We said a year before there were nine of them on the floor.

BRANNOCK
(quietly)
Eleven. I looked before I came up.

She goes in. She does not touch the bed. She goes to the bench, kneels, and reaches under it, to where an apprentice would know to look and nobody else would. She brings out a flat packet in oilcloth, tied with wire.

TAMSIN
She kept her real book where she kept her good chisels.

She unwraps it. A notebook, thick, soft at the corners. She opens it at the first page. It is nothing but tally-strokes: fives, gated, in a left-handed slant, row after row after row.

BRANNOCK
(over her shoulder)
What was she counting?

Tamsin turns pages. Strokes. More strokes. No words at all.

TAMSIN
Everything, by the look.

SMASH CUT TO:

MAIN TITLES — NINE CONCENTRIC RINGS DRAW THEMSELVES IN LIGHT, FIVE BRIGHT AND FOUR DARK. THE NOTE UNDER THEM IS 9.1717 Hz.

ACT ONE

INT. THE ARES FOUNDRY — THE COUNTING ROOM — MORNING

SUPER: "MARS. THE SAME WEEK."

A low room off the furnace hall, lined with ledgers: the foundry's books, spine after cracked spine, the oldest black with soot and age. A high desk. Brannock's own book, the current one, open on it with a pen in the gutter.

The notebook lies on the desk. Tamsin sits on a stool in front of it, chin on her fists. Brannock, standing, has been counting strokes with the back of a pen and writing subtotals on a slate.

BRANNOCK
Eleven thousand.

TAMSIN
Exactly?

BRANNOCK
Exactly. Not one over. She gates the last five and stops, and the rest of the book's blank but for the back page.

TAMSIN
(slowly)
Eleven thousand pours.

BRANNOCK
That's what he said. At the vault. The lad told us. "Eleven thousand pours. The cost was her."

TAMSIN
He was counting her up. Like a wage.
(she touches a row of strokes)
She wasn't keeping it for that. Nobody tallies their own pours, Bran. The book does that. You do that.

BRANNOCK
(nodding at the wall of ledgers)
I do, and it's eleven thousand and some. She poured for forty years. It'd be near enough.

TAMSIN
Near enough isn't exactly. She stopped on a gate.

She turns the notebook to the first page and puts her finger beside the first stroke. In the margin, tiny, the only writing in the whole run of tallies: a ledger reference. A shelf number and a line.

TAMSIN (CONT'D)
What's "Y1, 4"?

Brannock looks at it. Then he looks, with real reluctance, at the far end of the lowest shelf, where one ledger stands apart from the others in a tin sleeve.

BRANNOCK
Year One. Line four.
(a breath)
Nobody opens Year One. It's the foundry's birth. You look at it when you're made journeyman and then you put it back.

TAMSIN
Did you read it?

BRANNOCK
I looked at it. It's a list of what came in the gate.

TAMSIN
Then read me line four.

He fetches it. The sleeve comes off with a sound like a breath. The book inside is small and very black. He opens it flat on the desk and runs a thick finger down the first page.

BRANNOCK
(reading)
"Line one. Sand, river, forty loads. Line two. Charcoal. Line three. Tin, of Ceres."
(his finger stops)
"Line four. Bronze, received of the Regent, for the tongue. Thirty-one, and a little. Already alloyed. Not to be assayed."

TAMSIN
Not to be assayed.

BRANNOCK
That's what it says.

TAMSIN
A foundry that doesn't assay its bronze.

BRANNOCK
It's the Regent's order. It's got his mark. Ares Valorian.

They look at each other. Down the hall, the furnace draws, and for a second the flame in the counting-room lamp leans toward it.

INT. THE ARES FOUNDRY — THE PATTERN SHELF — DAY

An alcove behind the master's bench in the furnace hall: a shelf of old patterns and gauges, each on its nail. Wooden bell-profiles. A brass rule. And, hung by a loop of wire at the height of a child's hand, a small bronze disc, shining like a coin that has been in a pocket for a lifetime.

Tamsin lifts it off its nail. It is perfectly smooth on both sides.

TAMSIN
The luck piece.

BRANNOCK
Every apprentice rubs it. First day. I did. You did. He did.

TAMSIN
What is it?

BRANNOCK
It's the standard. The alloy the tongue's made of. You match a pour to that if you ever need to mend it.
(he hears himself)
It's the assay piece. For the bronze that wasn't to be assayed.

Tamsin turns the disc to the furnace light and tilts it. Smooth. Smooth. Then, at one low angle, a ghost: the faintest ring of a raised rim, and inside it nothing at all, a field worn flat.

TAMSIN
(very quietly)
There was something stamped on this.

BRANNOCK
There's a hole for a cord.

She finds it with her thumbnail: a small pierced hole at the edge, half-filled with twelve thousand years of polish. She holds the disc against her own collarbone, where a thing on a cord would hang.

TAMSIN
It's a tag, Bran. It's somebody's tag. A soldier's.

BRANNOCK
(hoarse)
And we rubbed it off.

ACT TWO

EXT. THE ARES FOUNDRY — THE YARD — DAY

Hard light. The new Aries bell hangs in its frame, still, the name on its lip. A ship is settling on the cinder apron beyond the gate, running lights on, waiting for permission.

SENTINEL (V.O.)
(from the gate-post, calm)
The Knight requests entry. He is carrying a letter.

Tamsin, in the yard with the disc in her fist, stares at the gate.

TAMSIN
He's carrying it?

KAEL MARREN comes through, pack on one shoulder, a folded paper held up like a pass.

KAEL
I wrote.

TAMSIN
You wrote and then you brought it.

KAEL
I wrote something in it.

She takes it. Opens it. Reads the single line. Whatever it says, she folds it very small and puts it inside her jacket, and then she hits him in the arm, hard, and then she puts the disc in his hand.

KAEL (CONT'D)
(looking at it)
The luck piece.

TAMSIN
Hold it where the light's low.

He does. He sees the rim. He sees the hole. His face changes slowly, the way it does when he hears the floor.

INT. THE ARES FOUNDRY — THE COUNTING ROOM — DAY

The three of them. Year One open on the desk. The notebook beside it. A fine balance, brass pans, set up by Brannock with the care of a man laying a table for a funeral. The disc lies in one pan.

BRANNOCK
Two and seven-tenths. Grams. Less what our thumbs took.

KAEL
And the tongue?

BRANNOCK
The book says thirty-one and a little. I weighed it myself when we hung it. Thirty kilos, eight hundred and some.

Kael is not good at sums. Tamsin is. She has the slate.

TAMSIN
(not looking up)
I've done it four ways. Allow for wear on the piece. Allow for dross in the melt. Allow for the "little".
(she turns the slate round)
It comes to eleven thousand four hundred, every way. Give or take six.

KAEL
Eleven thousand four hundred.

TAMSIN
She got to eleven thousand.

A silence. Kael picks up the notebook. Turns the pages of strokes. He understands it before either of them says it.

KAEL
(very quietly)
One pour each.

TAMSIN
Forty years. Every bell, every gear, every hinge that went out of this gate. She gave each one to one of them. In her head. And then a stroke in the book.

BRANNOCK
She never said.

TAMSIN
She said the cost before every pour. We thought she meant the pour.

Kael sets the notebook down as if it were hot.

KAEL
I rang it. In the vault. With both hands. And then I hung it in her bell.
(he looks at them)
I thought it was a piece of bronze that made a note.

From the furnace hall, through the open door, the fire draws once, deep, and a voice comes up out of it like heat off a road.

ARES (V.O.)
It is a piece of bronze that makes a note.

Brannock takes his cap off. Tamsin does not. Kael turns to the door.

KAEL
You knew.

ARES (V.O.)
I was there. Valorian came to me the morning after the signing with a sack he could barely lift. He said: they have signed under a number, and my dead are in it, and nobody will ever say them. He asked me for a way to make them heard.

KAEL
And you gave him a fire.

ARES (V.O.)
I gave him what I am. A list can be shut in a cabinet. A bell cannot be shut. Once in twelve years, twelve worlds would hear the Aries dead whether they wished to or not. I thought it the bravest thing a mortal ever asked of me.

TAMSIN
(flat; furious)
He melted their names.

ARES (V.O.)
Yes.

TAMSIN
So they'd be heard.

ARES (V.O.)
Yes.

TAMSIN
And for twelve thousand years everyone heard a nice note and thought it meant "we swore".

The fire is quiet for a moment. When the voice comes again there is something in it that Ares has not been heard to use before.

ARES (V.O.)
That was the flaw. I pressed Mars toward initiative. It did not occur to me that a man could act, greatly, and by the act make sure the thing was never done.

KAEL
(slowly)
Twelve Houses. Twelve tongues.

ARES (V.O.)
I answer for one House.

The fire sinks to its bank. He is gone. The question stays in the room.

ACT THREE

INT. THE ARES FOUNDRY — THE YARD — DUSK

The bell, dark against a red sky. A ladder against the frame. At the foot of it: Kael, with a jeweller's file and a fold of clean paper. Tamsin. Brannock, with the balance in its box under his arm.

KAEL
The Court will ask. Adaeze will ask, and she'll be right to. A worn disc and a dead woman's tally and an order not to look. That's a story. If the bronze in the tongue is the bronze in the tag, it's a fact.

TAMSIN
You'd have to cut it.

KAEL
A filing. Less than a grain.

TAMSIN
Out of them.

Kael has one hand on the ladder. He does not climb.

BRANNOCK
(gruff; it is the first time he has ever said it to anyone)
Say the cost.

Kael looks at him, surprised, and then not.

KAEL
If I file it, I've taken something off eleven thousand people who've had everything taken off them already, down to the stamp. And I'll have put a tool to the inside of her bell, that isn't to be touched till it's rung.
(a breath)
If I don't, it stays the foundry's secret. Like it was Valorian's. Like it was hers. And the next apprentice rubs the luck piece.

BRANNOCK
That's both sides. Which do you pay?

KAEL
(after a long moment)
The first. And I say sorry to them while I do it, which is no use to anybody, and I'll do it anyway.

He climbs. We stay on the ground with Tamsin and Brannock, looking up at the dark mouth of the bell. We hear nothing ring. We hear, very small, three strokes of a file, and Kael's voice, too low to make out, saying something that goes on longer than the filing does.

INT. THE ARES FOUNDRY — THE COUNTING ROOM — NIGHT

Lamp. The fold of paper open on the desk, a pinch of bright dust in its crease. Beside it a second pinch, from the rim of the disc. Brannock has an assayer's glass in his eye and two small crucibles on a spirit flame. He takes a long time. Nobody hurries him.

BRANNOCK
(at last, taking the glass out)
Same tin. Same lead. Same bit of something I've no name for.
(he sets the glass down)
It's the one metal.

Tamsin lets out a breath she has been holding since the pattern shelf. She picks up Orla's notebook and holds it against her chest.

TAMSIN
Four hundred and six.

KAEL
What?

TAMSIN
She was four hundred and six short. I pour now.

BRANNOCK
Tam.

TAMSIN
I'm not asking. I'll do the foundry's work and I'll give each pour to one of them, and I'll put my strokes after hers, and if it takes me till I'm her age it takes me till I'm her age.
(to Kael)
You said carrying has knees. You said that in your letter.

KAEL
(quietly)
I said it to Adaeze. I wrote it down after.

TAMSIN
Well. It was the only good line in it.

She opens the notebook to where the strokes stop, to begin her own. The blank pages run on under her thumb. She riffles them to the end, and stops.

TAMSIN (CONT'D)
There's the back page. Bran said there was a back page.

She turns it flat under the lamp. Kael leans in. Brannock comes round the desk.

INSERT — THE LAST PAGE OF THE NOTEBOOK

Ruled by hand, with a foundry straight-edge, into three columns. At the head of each, in Orla's left-handed slant: CLAUSE. TEXT. REASON.

Below, one line only, copied out in full: "1. No House shall bear arms within the orbit of another."

The third column beside it is empty, except that in the corner of the empty space, in pencil, pressed hard and then gone over again, are three words:

"Not a number."

At the foot of the page, a date. It is eleven years old.

Kael has gone white.

KAEL
That's the Schedule.

BRANNOCK
The what?

KAEL
The Accord's own book. The first page of it. It's in a locked cabinet in the Hall of the Seats and it has never left the room. Adaeze had never shown it to anyone.
(he touches the edge of the page, not the writing)
That's how it's ruled. That's the clause. That's where the blank is.

TAMSIN
She never went to Earth. Not once. She said the gravity was vulgar.

They stand over it: an exact copy, in a dead woman's hand, of a page she could not have seen.

CUT TO BLACK.

TAG

INT. THE ARES FOUNDRY — THE CASTING PIT — LATER THAT NIGHT

The pit, the bar of furnace light. A small mould is set in the black sand: nothing grand, a gate-hinge. Tamsin stands over it with the ladle, in Orla's apron, which is too long for her.

On the floor by the old dark stain: eleven beads of red, in a row.

TAMSIN
(to the mould; awkward; she has never done this aloud)
I don't know your name. I'm sorry. This one's yours.

She pours. Bronze runs gold into the sand. Steam.

In the dark at the edge of the pit the air shimmers red. The UNBLED stands there, stooped, a third gash on its arm shining wet. It is not watching Tamsin. It is watching the bronze cool.

On the floor, no twelfth bead comes.

Tamsin does not see the Shadow. She takes the notebook out of the apron pocket, licks the pencil the way Orla did, and under eleven thousand strokes in a left-handed slant makes one, upright, of her own.

CLOSING SEQUENCE — THE NINE RINGS FADE. A SINGLE LOW TONE, 9.1717 Hz, HOLDS UNDER THE CREDITS.

THE END OF EPISODE 104 (DRAFT 1 SHORT)
